      "torsemide",
      "ethacrynic acid"
    ],
    "mechanism": "Block the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter (NKCC2) in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle. Sodium and water loss is strong, and more calcium and magnesium are lost in the urine.",
    "pharmacokinetics": [
      "work within minutes when given IV",
      "oral furosemide is absorbed variably; bumetanide and torsemide are absorbed more reliably",
      "all are sulfonamides except ethacrynic acid"
    ],
    "indications": [
      "fluid overload in heart failure",
      "acute pulmonary edema",
      "edema from cirrhosis or nephrotic syndrome",
      "hyperkalemia"
    ],
    "adverse_effects": [
      "hypokalemia",
      "hypomagnesemia",
      "hypocalcemia",
      "volume depletion and prerenal kidney injury",
      "ototoxicity",
      "metabolic alkalosis",
      "hyperuricemia and gout"
    ],
    "contraindications": [
      "anuria",
      "severe volume depletion"
    ],
    "interactions": [
      "aminoglycosides add to ototoxicity",
      "raise lithium levels",
      "digoxin toxicity is more likely with low potassium or magnesium"
    ],
    "monitoring": [
      "potassium, magnesium and creatinine",
      "daily weight and fluid balance"
    ],
    "overdose": {
      "features": [
        "dehydration",
        "hypotension",
        "electrolyte loss"
      ],
      "treatment": [
        "IV fluids and electrolyte replacement"
      ]
    },
    "confused_with": [],
    "pearls": [
      "Loops lose calcium, thiazides keep it: loop diuretics cause hypocalcemia, thiazides cause hypercalcemia.",
      "Ethacrynic acid is the loop diuretic without a sulfonamide group, used when a sulfa reaction is a concern.",
      "Loop diuretics cause a hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, also called contraction alkalosis."
    ],
